The '''Ring of Nestor'''{{Efn|The name "Ring of Nestor" was invented by Arthur Evans in the 1920s.{{sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=6}} It is sometimes referred to as ''CMS'' VI.229, after its number in the {{ill|Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel|de|lt=''Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel''}} (''CMS'').{{sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}} }} is a gold [[signet ring]] first described by the archaeologist [[Arthur Evans]] in 1925. According to Evans, it was made on [[Minoan Crete]] in the Late Bronze Age and discovered in a [[Tholos tomb|Mycenaean tholos tomb]] near the ancient site of [[Palace of Nestor|Pylos]] in [[Messenia]], in southwest Greece. The ring has a crowded and complex design, including human figures as well as real and mythical animals, and has been interpreted as indicating aspects of [[Minoan religion]]. Evans considered it to show scenes from the [[underworld]], and to illustrate connections between Minoan belief and [[ancient Egyptian religion]]. It is currently held by the [[Ashmolean Museum]] at the [[University of Oxford]], to which Evans gifted it in 1939.

Since its discovery, the ring's authenticity has been debated, with several scholars considering it a modern fake, and others defending it as a genuinely ancient artefact. Arguments in both directions have been made on the grounds of iconographical consistency with other Minoan artworks, and based upon the potential role of  [[Émile Gilliéron fils|Émile Gilliéron ''fils'']], an art restorer, suspected forger, and prolific collaborator of Evans's, in the ring's discovery. Other arguments for authenticity include the wear evident upon the ring and microscopic analysis of its engraving technique, while circumstantial evidence against it has been adduced from Evans's convoluted story of its origins and the fortuitous connections between its iconography and Evans's own ideas about the links between Minoan and Egyptian religion. The ring was removed from display at the Ashmolean in the 20th century, but returned to public view in 1995.

== Description ==
The authenticity of the Ring of Nestor is disputed.{{Sfn|Hudler|2019|p=4}} Scholars who consider the ring to be ancient date it to the [[Late Minoan|Late Minoan&nbsp;I]] period ({{Circa|1700|1450 BCE}}), during the [[Neopalatial Period|Neopalatial period]] of Minoan civilization.{{Sfn|Galanakis|2013|p=159}} The ring is made of gold of 97% purity,{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=223}} with the remaining 3% being made up of copper,{{Sfn|Galanakis|2013|p=159}} and weighs {{Convert|31.76|g|oz|sigfig=3}}.{{sfnm|1a1=Galanakis|1y=2013|1p=159|2a1=Hughes-Brock|2a2=Boardman|2y=2018|2p=448}} It has a hollow [[bezel]],{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}} made of a gold sheet {{Convert|0.25|mm|in|2|abbr=on}} in thickness,{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=8}} with a groove for a finger set into the underside. The ring's hoop is decorated with a central band of [[Embossing (manufacturing)|embossed]] hemispherical pieces,{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}} which are [[Soldering|soldered]] onto the hoop,{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=9}} and with [[Granulation (jewellery)|granules]] of two different sizes. These pieces and granules have been deformed, with the most damage apparent on the central band of large hemispheres.{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}} 

The hoop is slightly elliptical, with internal diameters of {{Convert|1.4|and|1.56|cm|in|3|abbr=on}}, a width of {{Convert|0.82|cm|in|2|abbr=on}}, and a thickness of {{Convert|0.35|cm|in|2|abbr=on}}. The bezel is {{Convert|3.29|cm|in|3|abbr=on}} horizontally and {{Convert|2.18|cm|in|3|abbr=on}} vertically.{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}} Owing to the narrow diameter of the hoop, [[Arthur Evans]] (who first introduced the ring to scholarship) considered that it was probably intended to be hung on a string rather than worn on the finger.{{Sfn|Evans|1930|p=146}} 

The iconography on the ring's bezel is divided into four scenes, each occupying a quarter of the face. The scenes are separated by a thicker vertical band and a thinner horizontal band; the vertical band rises out of a base. At the intersection of the vertical band and the base is an animal form, whose identification has been debated. The bands are generally taken to represent the trunk and branches of a large tree.{{Sfn|Nilsson|1927|pp=549–550}} The bands were probably made with a [[Punch (tool)|punch]], while the figures are likely to have been both punched and [[Engraving|engraved]].{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=8}}  

Of the 99 known signet rings from the Aegean Bronze Age, the Ring of Nestor shows the greatest number of individual figures: it depicts fourteen human figures and at least five other animals.{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=228}} In the scene in the upper-left quadrant,<!-- The CMS gives the directions on the *sealing* (impression), and therefore calls this the top right -- see photo on p. 449 --> a lion can be seen lying on a large structure, called a "table" by Helen Hughes-Brock and [[John Boardman (art historian)|John Boardman]], beneath which are two naked human women, each with one hand on the table. Four small branches, resembling [[ivy]], sprout from the vertical dividing-band to the image's right. Below this scene, several women in long, layered skirts can be seen: one walks towards the central band, while two approach a small table on which a [[griffin]] is seated. The women may have the heads of birds, or alternatively simply have their heads leant forwards. Behind the griffin, a female figure stands in a skirt slightly longer than those of the others, facing the ring's centre. Hughes-Brock and Boardman describe these two scenes as "otherworldly", in contrast to the scenes on the ring's right, which only contain human figures.{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|pp=448–449}}

{{Multiple image
| image1            = 'Procession Fresco' from Knossos, Neopalacial period, 1600 - 1450 BC, Heraklion Archaeological Museum - 32931077207 crop.jpg
| image2            = Cueilleuse de safran, fresque, Akrotiri, Grèce.jpg
| alt2              = Ancient wall-painting: a woman, skin rendered in white, stoops to gather flowers: her hair is elaborately made up and she wears a many-layered skirt
| footer            = Minoan frescoes showing examples of clothing similar to that depicted on the Ring of Nestor
| alt1              = Ancient wall painting: male figure, bare chested and wearing a kilt, carries a jug to the right
