On the right-hand side of the bezel, the upper image shows a pair of women, one sitting and one squatting, facing each other: one may be wearing a skirt, or both may be naked. Next to them are a man and a woman, standing; the man wears a belted [[kilt]] and the woman a knee-length layered skirt. Above the human figures can be seen two winged insects and two ovoid shapes identified by Hughes-Brock and Boardman as [[Pupa|pupae]]. Below this scene is one of four human figures walking towards the centre of the ring: the first and last figures are men, and the two in the middle are women in long, flounced skirts.{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|pp=448–449}}

===Interpretation===

Evans considered the ring to depict scenes from the Minoan [[underworld]],{{Sfn|Evans|1925|p=51}} specifically "the admission of the departed into the realms of bliss".{{Sfn|Evans|1930|p=145}} He interpreted the shape dividing the four quarters of the ring as "unquestionably a tree, old and gnarled", which he considered to be comparable with the [[world tree]] called [[Yggdrasil]] in [[Norse mythology]].{{Sfn|Evans|1925|pp=50–51}} He considered the lion to be "the guardian of the [[Underworld|Lower World]]",{{sfn|Evans|1925|p=14}} and the creature at the base, which he took to be a dog, to be "the Minoan forerunner of [[Cerberus]]"{{Sfn|Evans|1925|p=51}}{{Spaced en dash}}the three-headed dog who guards the underworld in classical Greek mythology.{{Sfn|Ogden|2013|p=109}} The griffin on the bezel is a common motif in Minoan art, originally taken from the Near East.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Galanakis|2013|p=159}}. On the origins of the griffin motif, see {{harvnb|Caubet|2002|p=229}}.}}

Evans wrote in a letter, shortly after seeing the ring, that it depicted "a map of the [[Elysium|Elysian Fields]]" according to the Minoans.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=6}} He interpreted the winged insects as butterflies, and as symbols of the [[reincarnation]] of the human beings next to them. In the same scene, he saw the standing female figure as a goddess, and the male figure as a young man reunited with his lover, either after death or possibly upon entering the underworld while alive.{{Sfn|Evans|1930|pp=151–152}} He considered this goddess to be a [[Chthonic deities|deity of the underworld]], the lion (following parallels in Egyptian iconography) to be associated with her, and the two women in the same scene to be her handmaidens.{{refn|{{harvnb|Evans|1930|pp=153–154}}; {{harvnb|Evans|1925|pages=65–68}} (on the lion's Egyptian connection).}} He described the scene with the griffin as that of souls being tested on entry to "the Halls of the Just in the Griffin's Court", and the griffin itself as a "Chief Inquisitor".{{Sfn|Evans|1930|p=154}} Evans characterised the whole scene as "the abode of light rather than of darkness", equivalent to the paradise of Elysium in later Greek mythology.{{Sfn|Evans|1930|pp=155–156}} [[Nanno Marinatos]] and Briana Jackson suggest that Evans's interpretation of the scene via Egyptian mythology, as well as his reading of the central motif as a tree, was influenced by James Frazer's ''[[The Golden Bough]]'', as well as by the discovery of the [[Tomb of Tutankhamun]] in 1923.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|pp=9–10}}[[File:Ring of Nestor Cast Evans 1925.png|thumb|alt=Grey photograph of the cast of the ring's impression: as infobox image, except the creature at the base of the tree is highly indistinct|Photograph of a cast of the ring's impression, published by Evans in 1925]]
[[Martin P. Nilsson]] discussed the ring in a 1927 work on Minoan and Mycenaean religion, in which he called it "a most amazing find ... [which] differs most markedly from other Mycenaean rings".{{Refn|{{harvnb|Nilsson|1927|p=549}}. Nilsson's treatment of the ring is discussed by Marinatos.{{sfn|Marinatos|2015|p=191}}.}}{{Efn|In the early twentieth century, the term "Mycenaean" could be applied to the civilisation of Crete: see {{harvnb|Karadimas|Momigliano|2004|p=252}}.}} Nilsson disagreed with Evans's association between the tree motif and the world tree, pointing out that the motif was not known to be attested prior to the eighth century&nbsp;CE; he considered the tree more likely to prefigure the tree in the [[Garden of the Hesperides]] in Greek mythology, which grew golden apples.{{Sfn|Nilsson|1927|p=550}} In support of this, he suggested that the creature labelled by Evans as a proto-Cerberus should be considered a forerunner of [[Ladon (mythology)|Ladon]], the dragon who guarded that tree.{{Sfn|Nilsson|1927|p=551}} He considered there to be no compelling reason to suggest that the scenes on the ring were related to the afterlife or the underworld.{{Sfn|Nilsson|1927|p=556}} Galanakis identifies the figure as a dragon, and considers it to be inspired by similar [[Babylon|Babylonian]] motifs otherwise rare in Minoan art;{{Refn|{{harvnb|Galanakis|2013|p=159}}. On the origins of the griffin motif, see {{harvnb|Caubet|2002|p=229}}.}} Hughes-Brock and Boardman call it a "Minoan dragon".{{Sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=449}} Andreas Vlachopoulos identifies it as a scorpion.{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=228}}

{{ill|Jean-Claude Poursat|fr}}, in 1976, suggested that the vertical and horizontal bands represented rivers, a hypothesis initially entertained but dismissed by Evans. In support of this, Poursat argued that the creature at the base was a "fantastical crocodile" or "crocodile-dragon". He further suggested that the lower half of the bezel, assumed to be a single episode by Evans, may have been intended to show two successive scenes.{{Sfn|Poursat|1976|p=472}} Olga Krzyszkowska described Evans's interpretation of the bezel as an underworld scene as "fanciful" in 2005.{{Sfn|Krzyszkowska|2005|p=334}}

== History ==

[[File:Ring of Nestor Side Evans 1925.png|thumb|upright=0.7|alt=Line drawing of a gold ring, in three different orientations|Line drawing of the ring from the side, published by Evans in 1925{{sfn|Evans|1925|p=47}}]]

In 1907, a series of [[tholos tomb|tholos tombs]] at [[Kakovatos]] in [[Messenia]], in the southwest [[Peloponnese (region)|Peloponnese]] region of Greece, were excavated by the German archaeologist [[Wilhelm Dörpfeld]].{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|pp=224–225}} According to Evans, the ring was taken by a local peasant from one of these tombs (along with blocks of masonry) prior to the excavation, probably from the grave-pit inside the tomb's chamber.{{Sfn|Evans|1925|p=46}}{{Efn|Evans considered the ring to have come from "Tomb A" at Kakovatos.{{sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=224}}}}

In 1924, or possibly earlier, the ring was shown to the acquisitions committee of the [[National Archaeological Museum, Athens|National Archaeological Museum]] in Athens, of which the German archaeologist [[Georg Karo]] was a member.{{Sfn|Marinatos|2015|p=194}} Following Karo's judgement, the committee rejected the ring as a forgery, created by a modern goldsmith in the early twentieth century.{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=226}} Evans was subsequently informed of the ring's existence by somebody he described as "a trustworthy source" (and later "a friend"): Karo inferred that Evans was referring to [[Émile Gilliéron fils|Émile Gilliéron ''fils'']], an art restorer, suspected forger, and prolific collaborator of Evans's.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Marinatos|2015|pages=194–195}}. On Gilliéron's potential illicit activities, see {{harvnb|Lapatin|2002|pages=144{{ndash}}150, 171}}; and {{harvnb|Gere|2009|p=128}}.}} Nanno Marinatos endorsed this view in 2015,{{Sfn|Marinatos|2015|pp=194–195}} and Vlachopoulos called it "very possible" in 2020.{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=225}}    

Evans subsequently travelled to the Peloponnese to view the ring, where he was told that the finder had died, leaving the ring to his son; the son passed it into the possession of the owner of a nearby vineyard.{{Sfn|Evans|1925|p=46}} Evans purchased the ring from the vineyard owner between 1924 and 1925,{{Sfnm|1a1=Evans|1y=1925|1p=46|2a1=Vlachopoulos|2y=2020|2p=225}} and named it the "Ring of Nestor" after [[Nestor (mythology)|the mythological king]] of [[Messenia (ancient region)|the region of Messenia]] in the [[Homer]]ic poems.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=6}}  He gifted the ring to the [[Ashmolean Museum]] at the [[University of Oxford]] in 1939.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=6}} It was subsequently removed from display, though placed back there in 1995.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=1}} As of 2018, it remains in the Ashmolean.{{sfn|Hughes-Brock|Boardman|2018|p=448}}

== Debate over authenticity ==
The authenticity of the Ring of Nestor has been debated since its introduction to scholarship. In 2019, Angelika Hudler categorised the ring among {{Lang|la|gemmae dubitandae}} ({{Gloss|jewels deserving scepticism}}).{{Efn|According to Hudler, the term "attempts to include artifacts of unknown provenance and date of production into the scientific discussion while at the same time stressing the doubtful character of their alleged Aegean Bronze Age origin".{{sfn|Hudler|2019|p=1}}}}{{Sfn|Hudler|2019|p=4}} In 2013, Yannis Galanakis called the ring "one of the most controversial Aegean objects in the Ashmolean's collection".{{Sfn|Galanakis|2013|p=159}}

=== Arguments against authenticity ===
Before the 1970s, most scholars considered the ring a forgery.{{Sfn|Eliopoulos|2013|p=210}} Nilsson, who treated the ring as genuine in 1927, revised the 1950 edition of his work to call it a "suspect object".{{Sfn|Hudler|2019|p=6}}  According to [[Nanno Marinatos]], her father [[Spyridon Marinatos]] considered the Ring of Nestor a fake. She cites as evidence a reference in a 1928 letter by Marinatos to "reprimands" Evans was receiving about forgeries. Hagen Biesantz argued in 1954 that the ring's iconography was inconsistent with the known corpus of [[Minoan art]], and accordingly that the ring was a forgery.{{Refn|Cited in {{harvnb|Hudler|2019|p=6}}.}}

[[File:Epiphany_cycle_—_THE_RING_OF_MINOS.jpg|thumb|The Ring of Minos|alt=A gold ring, like the Ring of Nestor, with an oval bezel inscribed with figures. A youth climbs a hill in the centre to pick something from a tree, while two seated female figures watch.]]
Evans published the ring alongside the "Thisbe Treasure" from [[Boeotia]], considered a modern forgery, and the connection has cast doubt upon the Ring of Nestor in turn.{{Sfn|Krzyszkowska|2005|p=334}} John Younger, in 1975, suggested that the ring had been forged by Gilliéron ''fils'' and [[Émile Gilliéron|his father]], who had also been accused of forging the [[Ring of Minos]] apparently found at Knossos and also published by Evans.{{refn|{{harvnb|Pini|1998|p=1}}. On the authenticity of the Ring of Minos, see {{harvnb|Krzyszkowska|2005|pages=334–335}}.}} In 1986,{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=1}} the Swedish archaeologist {{ill|Robin Hägg|sv}} separately reported hearing from a collaborator of [[Axel W. Persson]], who had worked on the Aegean Bronze Age in the 1930s, that Persson had told her that Gilliéron ''fils'' had forged the Ring of Nestor: Hägg subsequently heard the same story from the Egyptologist [[Torgny Säve-Söderbergh]] and from [[Doro Levi]], director of the [[Italian School of Archaeology at Athens]] from 1947 until 1976.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=11}}. For Levi's dates as director, see {{cite web| title=Direttori| lang=it| url=https://www.scuoladiatene.it/saia/storia/i-direttori.html| publisher=Italian School of Archaeology at Athens| access-date=2025-08-07}}}} [[Agnes Sakellariou]] upheld the Gilliéron hypothesis in 1994, arguing that the insects labelled as butterflies by Evans had no direct parallels in Minoan art, but could have been based by a forger on Mycenaean examples found in tombs on the Greek mainland.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|pp=3–5}} Krzyszkowska similarly accused the Gilliérons of forging the ring in 2005.{{Sfn|Krzyszkowska|2005|loc=p. 336, n. 113}}

Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson wrote in 2011 that the "complicated chain of transmission" reported to Evans raised suspicion, and made it impossible to verify the truth of the ring's discovery.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=7}} They also argued that butterflies had no connection to the soul or reincarnation in Minoan culture, but that they were included on the ring because they held this metaphorical function in Victorian thought, and had been incorrectly linked to the concept in Mycenaean culture by [[Heinrich Schliemann]] and more widely in Frazer's work.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|pp=11–12}} Marinatos and Jackson suggested that the connections between the ring's design and Egyptian iconography were based directly on ''The Golden Bough'', which emphasised the religious role of oak trees and [[mistletoe]], and calculated to appeal to Evans, who had long sought to establish a relationship between Minoan religion and that of ancient Egypt.{{Sfn|Marinatos|Jackson|2011|p=10}}

=== Arguments in favour of authenticity ===
[[V. Gordon Childe]] wrote in 1925 that "fortunately no such suspicions [of forgery] attach to the unique gold ring", and that it was "undoubtedly a remnant of the regal wealth once buried in the great tombs of Kakovatos".{{Sfn|Childe|1925|p=636}}{{Efn|Joseph Alexander MacGillivray suggests that Childe's enthusiasm for the ring came "because he was himself busily myth-making in his own right" with regard to the origins of the [[Indo-Europeans]].{{sfn|MacGillivray|2000|page=289}}}} Yiannis Sakellarakis, in 1973, considered the iconography of the ring to match other known Minoan examples, and so that the ring was likely to be genuine.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Sakellarakis|1973}}, cited in {{harvnb|Marinatos|2015|at=p. 194, n. 27}}.}} Jean-Claude Poursat accepted these arguments in his own publication of 1976.{{Sfn|Poursat|1976|p=472}}

[[File:Ring_of_Nestor_drawing_Gillieron.png|thumb|alt=Colourful drawing in the style of a Minoan fresco, replicating the bezel of the Ring of Nestor. The creature at the bottom of the tree is rendered as an oddly-posed dog.|Gilliéron's rendering of the ring's impression in the style of a Minoan fresco, published by Evans in 1930]]
Vlachopoulos suggests that Gilliéron could not have been involved in faking the ring, because his drawing of the bezel for Evans's publications showed a hound for the creature Vlachopoulos identified as a scorpion, demonstrating "his ignorance and his innocence".{{Sfn|Vlachopoulos|2020|p=228}} {{ill|Ingo Pini|de}} similarly argued that the different treatment of the creature on the ring and Gilliéron's later painting of the same scene, in which Pini described the rendering as "a rather odd-looking quadruped", indicates that Gilliéron could not have made both.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=6}}

Similarly, Theodore Eliopoulos suggested that the "table" on which the lion reclines as a horizontal platform supported by three incurved altars: such a platform was unknown in Minoan iconography at the time of the ring's discovery,{{Sfn|Eliopoulos|2013|pp=212–214}} but later found in the 1970s and 1980s on frescoes from [[Akrotiri (prehistoric city)|Akrotiri]]. Eliopoulos considers this evidence for a genuine Bronze Age origin.{{Sfn|Eliopoulos|2013|p=221}} Similarly, he argues that the appearance of lions as supporters of goddesses, known from Akrotiri frescoes as well as rings and sealings discovered after 1925, suggest the authenticity of the ring, as the lion's presence was considered anomalous when the ring was first published.{{Sfn|Eliopoulos|2013|p=219}} Pini argued against Sakellariou's interpretation of the butterflies as without precedent, suggesting that they echoed sealings found at the Cretan site of [[Hagia Triada]], excavated in 1902–1903 but not published until 1925.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=5}} He also drew a parallel between the creature at the base of the tree, which he called a "Minoan dragon", and a form on a seal found at [[Mycenae]] in the 1990s, while arguing that the motif would not have been known to a forger in the early twentieth century.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=6}}

In 1998, [[Jack Ogden (jewellery historian)|Jack Ogden]] studied the manufacturing technique of the ring, and concluded that it matched that of genuine examples, whereby the bezel was carved first, the hoop added, and the back plate of the bezel then soldered into place. In addition, he considered that microscopic analysis of the engraving showed that it had been done according to Mycenaean techniques, which would have been unknown to an early twentieth-century forger.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Ogden|1998|p=18}}, cited in {{harvnb|Craddock|2009|p=375}}.}} Ultrasound scans of the ring presented by Walter Müller in 2002 <!-- Not a typo: Muller 2003 is the record of a conference in 2002. -->confirmed that it was hollow, dispelling a longstanding belief that it was solid in construction, which would be unusual for a genuinely Bronze Age ring.{{Sfnm|1a1=Müller|1y=2003|1p=480|2a1=Krzyszkowska|2a2=2005|2loc=p. 334, n. 111}} Pini suggested that the wear on the decorations of the hoop indicates intensive use, and parallels other rings known to be genuine.{{Sfn|Pini|1998|p=10}} Childe similarly suggested that the wear on these parts may suggest that the ring was actually worn, in contrast to Evans's belief.{{Sfn|Childe|1925|p=636}} 

Kate Cooper, in 2021, wrote that the ring was generally considered fake, but that the 2015 discovery of a ring (designated Ring 2) in the [[Tomb of the Griffin Warrior]], a [[shaft grave]] at Pylos dating to the [[Late Helladic]] IIA period ({{Circa|1400|1300 BCE}}) with similar iconography, particularly the ivy, pointed towards its authenticity.{{Refn|{{harvnb|Cooper|2021|p=92}}, citing {{harvnb|Davis|Stocker|2016|at=p. 648, n. 77}}. For the absolute dates, see {{harvnb|Shelmerdine|2008|p=4}}. On Ring 2 from the Tomb of the Griffin Warrior, see {{harvnb|Yamasaki|2022|at=pp. 98–99, with fig. 4}}.}}

